    "turn_text": "who Doug Collins is? Well, I can't. I appreciate it. Thanks for being here. The book was almost in a way that sort of cathartic, you know, coming through everything we came through. You were there. I was there for that whole time, but it also comes from our background and I love the way you put lens. And from my lens, it was being raised in North Georgia, a little place called Gainesville, which has grown a little bit over time. But we're right at the foothills of the Appalachian Mountains. I'm about 15, 20 minutes from the start of the Appalachian Trail. It's an area that over time has grown, the lake, Atlantis pushed up. But it's always growing up. Especially when my dad got transferred to Gainesville, before I was born and raised there, Gainesville was still a small place. It was still more Appalachia than it was definitely Atlanta. And so those kind of values just came up. So myself, my brother, my mom, my mom worked with senior adults as we were growing up and in the home. It's sort of interesting to me. A lot of people have these backgrounds where you didn't know what you didn't have because we had everything we thought we needed. And that was sort of our background. And my dad, who is still alive, by the way, and he's a state trooper for over 31 years, when he retired, I get worth it, work ethic. When he retired, he had almost two years of unused sick leave and vacation that he just went to work every day, whether he was it. And that sort of embedded into me. So as I was growing up, went to North Georgia College, went to, got, thought I was going to the law school, thought I was going to be the, you know, the politician going to be the lawyer going to that. And that was back in the time, you remember the old show LA law. Well, everybody wanted to be a lawyer at that point. So I didn't get anywhere I wanted to. So I was getting ready at that point, or a little bit later, is getting married. And Lisa and I have been married now for 33 years. And it started a journey. And some people asked me, how did I get there? Well, coming from a state troopers kid and when we didn't have a lot. I mean, when we went to on vacation, we went to the state parks because we got a discount because that's where we went. So for me, it was growing up with books. It was growing up with the radio. That's how I sort of saw the world. Never thinking that I might one day be able to travel the world or sit in Congress. Where you come from, it's a matter of lens and it was for us, you know, you had church, you had your faith, you had that background of belief, you know, we argued about football and politics. It became something that was a part of you. Being a part of your community was never in doubt for me because that's just, you know, just what you did. And as I grew, Sort of in my life and faith, I sort of struggled about like a lot of young people did. I knew there was a calling in my life and faith, but I sort of run from it a little bit until one day answering that call became, as after college, I've been in business for a few years. and was doing okay, but just something missing. And so when I answered that call of faith, I started getting my master's of divinity and started as a youth pastor, then I became a pastor for over 11 years. Thought that that was probably where I was going to be. I was going to be a pastor and that was good. I was enjoying it. And then had spent a little time in the Navy at that point as a chaplain had gotten out. I have a daughter who has spina bifida and Jordan is now almost 30. You met Jordan. She's came up before him. So she had a lot of surgeries early. She can't walk. She's but she's working now to lives at home with us. So we didn't get to stay in the Navy like I thought we might have got out and stayed at my church. And then in 2002 got back in the United States Air Force. And I've been there ever since. I'm still there. Even while I was serving in Congress was a part of the Air Force that I went to Iraq in 2008. That sort of built in that experience of service from my dad and sort of built into my service on how I saw Congress and how we worked. But then about 11 years in a pastorate, I just sensed it was another time to change, which then led me to law school. So you talk about getting some interesting comments. You go from a pastor to a lawyer. I got a lot of weird things. People saying, that's just strange. How can you be a pastor and a lawyer? In the South, even more so. But as I always told them, I said from my perspective, pastor and lawyers were the same thing. We looked at somebody and we heard about what their situation was. We told them the worst possible scenario and gave them the best possible answer. So, and then that led to an open seat in the Georgia House, which I served for six years in the Georgia House that I represented, and then came up to Washington, D.C. And so, for this troopers kid, and I use that term a lot, and I use it as a term of respect for my dad. as a troopers kid to sit in the halls of Congress and to one day be able to write a book with the first-hand experience of some of the most amazing, and I say that not in positive terms always, some of the most interesting times that we lived through in the last few years. That's just who I am. I'm Lisa's husband. I'm father to three kids. But yet it was sitting front road to some of the most incredible towns we've seen in the last number of years in our country. So",
