    "turn_text": "what struck you? It did a little bit. It's funny you should say about walking down the street. We had a next door neighbor back in my home. And I actually have moved back very close to my dad. I'm about 100 yards, but we're sort of two different areas of this little peninsula. We had a lady named Betty West with my next door neighbor. We didn't need an alarm system when I was growing up. We didn't, we left the doors open all the time because Miss Betty watched everything we did my brother and I and one day I remember Miss Betty came to my dad. We were coming back home from somewhere and dad was driving and we got out of the truck and it was back when I first started driving and Miss Betty came out and she asked my dad Leonard. She said Leonard was Douglas in a hurry yesterday because when those rocks were spraying out from one of the car when he left the dirt road I said really, really. I mean, it was just that subtle southern, you know, just that southern way of saying, you know, Douglas, you know, laid out, you know, black marks going out of the dirt road yesterday. So yes, I know that feeling. And yes, it did. And I think that's the part when you grow up and your background of books and reading and thinking about Washington DC and thinking about legislating. You know, it's really tried, but it's really true. The Mr. Smith goes to Washington mentality. This place is a special place. Those of us who come to Washington DC have a, I believe, a trust with those who've went before, those from all the way up from the starting our country to now, to be what we are called and that's legislators. And to get up here and to, and one of the things we did and I worked with you on many things, criminal justice reform, music modernization, you know, the, the interesting with, you know, the, how we deal with cloud computing and law enforcement and all those big ideas to get those done, but to see that that wasn't valued by everybody, that it was not about legislating, it was about being a congressperson. And I think that from my background, and I appreciate you, it's really interesting you should bring that up. That's what has always got me about, and during this time, especially this very difficult time, is we were dealing in stuff that was coming from a passionate side that was not, in my opinion, helping anyone. It was designed to, I frankly guess, we'll get into it later, get at it, a president they didn't like. But also just looking around and Ken, I think you've seen this a little bit as well. Why are we here? If you're a legislator and never have legislated, are we missing the point? And I think from my perspective, that is that background that I brought is I believe that you came to do something, not be something. And that's just sort of where I was at. And not to be critical of any particular person, but that's the culture of Washington today.",
