    "turn_text": "Well, it was amazing. a person who will remain nameless. You know, being in the military, you know, we always have the National Defense Authorization Act. We have bills coming up. And there was a bill, there was a part of a bill on funding. It was in our appropriation side. I don't remember the OCO funding, which was the overseas contingency amount that we were spending on the global world terrorism. Well, in my opinion, even being in the military, that should have been at some point absorbed back into the regular budget. We don't have time to talk about the budget anymore. But I was going to vote against this member. Mick Mulvaney, myself, was only about three of us who were going to vote against increasing this budget. And I had somebody come to me. I remember on the floor coming to me and said, why aren't, you know, where are you going to do this? I said, I'm voting no. And I said, you should too. And they said, and they stopped for me and they said, yeah, it's a bad vote. They said, but I've not been in the military like you and I can't defend you. I thought to myself, have we missed it so bad that you can't explain good votes or bad votes that because of the perception that is out there that they feel like they can't control?",
