  {
    "episode_id": 90943,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90943.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 25,
    "turn_text": "Well, it was really interesting because you and I being on the judiciary committee time. That was the last two years of Bob Goodlatte. In 2017, Chairman Goodlatte was the chairman of our judiciary committee. And I can remember that was the year Jamie Raskin came in, Freeman Jaapal came in, there was a new group that came in, Jerry Nadler of course was still there, and he later on became our chairman. But what I saw from day one the 2017 was it was just a constant fight. And it was never really about the bill in front of us. It was about the Trump business. It was about Trump doing this. It was like every time, and we would spend hours. I can remember going back to the conference room and sitting there waiting, saying eventually they're going to stop talking so we could finish up what we were doing. It was just a different tenor. It was almost like we're going to set stage anything we can do. And I write in the book, and I think this is important, and I talked about it even back then. They couldn't undo 2016. They couldn't get it out of their head. But what they wanted to do was harass President Trump as far as as much as they could, they had at the point because of the Russia investigation at this point, the emails, the Russia investigation coming forward. And remember, it seems like years ago now, but it's just 2017. The first five months of 2017 went from, you had the Flynn issue, you had the Comey, you had all of this happening, and then by the first part of the, by the late spring of 2017, to the point where Comey was fired, Mueller was appointed a special counsel, and that's then they had their hook. That was the hook that they hung in on. Even some Republicans, or if you remember, they said, well, leave Mueller alone, let him finish it. So this went and began to fester for almost two years. And then in 2018, after everything, I mean, President Trump had been called everything. Whether you liked President Trump, disliked President Trump. For a year and a half it was the drum beat of being, you know, bigoted, racist.",
