  {
    "episode_id": 90943,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90943.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 59,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, that was one of the things that I want people to gain from this book, and if they read it, is not just, and some people have said it was just a regurgitate. It's a Trump version of the impeachment. No, I want you to hear me. We're going to present what we saw as the facts and from a Republican perspective. That's true. But what also was the underlying theme of why I wanted to write this book, because I believe that the press, although we talked to them about it, they were never picking this up because they only wanted the narrative of an impeachment. They only wanted the narrative of something that was sexier, I guess is the best way to put it. The real problem here was the procedural rules and the way that they were being overrun. And the fact that, how many hearings that we had were Jerry Nadler just frankly lost control of the committee. He would not recognize people for motions, especially our side, would let his side go over, would cut our people off, would call Debbie Lesco, one of our colleagues, her arguments are ridiculous and basically stupid. And we just had these fights. with jim since a runner who had been chairman of the committee you know another and was trying to point stuff out and in that it would just jerry and i would just run over him because he was i think was under so much pressure and also He wanted to be chairman, but there's a mentality being chairman, and he just, and running a committee, especially a contentious committee, was not there. So we looked like a circus for the first seven, eight months of, but I also talk about the fact that we as a majority, all we had was what they had had for, you know, for eight years is the ability to bring issues up. At the end of the day, they could vote us down. I never could get that through to Jerry Nadler. Jerry, just let us have our time. Let us make our points. Let us make our motions. And then you can move on. During one of the gun bills, we had about a hundred members. He finally looked at me and said, you're not going to make any more men. We're going to shut this down. And I said, I had to point out that his rush to get the bill to the committee, you can't do that. So they had to redo the thing right in the middle of the committee. But that's what we saw. So, people on the floor, I don't know if you experienced this, that I did, I write about what Democrats were coming up to me on the floor and said, what are y'all doing over there? This is an embarrassment. So, by the time it came to this phone call, you talk about, I love how you phrase this. Adler and Pelosi, two styles, and the judiciary had become a joke. It really, unfortunately had one of the oldest, most prestigious committees in the house, but it just had turned into a joke. and then you have Adam Schiff, the California and the slick talking smooth operator intelligence committee chairman at the time who was very close to Pelosi still is, had came up with this and we already knew he was actually should be a fact witness in this because him and his staff had contact with this whistleblower of this phone call that was supposedly so bad. He convinced, I believe, and had presented it to the leadership in such a way that let me handle this. You know, let's move this out of the judiciary. If you were going to ever do impeachment, it had to be in the judiciary, but for the first time ever, really an impeachment proceeding was done by another committee and it was done a lot because of style, because Schiff was willing to run a hard-line gavel, so to speak, over a committee and not let it get out of hand. And so I think that was a part of what happened in the fall. But what it did to us was take the very committee that should have been a process, that should have been investigating, that should have been doing this, we were completely sidelined. And it was because of frankly the rivalry that developed between Chef and Nadler and Chef 1. I've always said this though about this, the number 24. If Nancy Pelosi had waited 24 hours, I've always wondered, let, because she announced on one day that we're going to do this, the next day the president released the full transcript. And he was saying he was going to when she announced it. If she had just waited 24 hours to let the transcript get out there, I always have at least the seed of doubt would she have went forward. Probably so, but at least she would have seen the document, you know, the world would have seen the document and could have made a judgment about it.",
