  {
    "episode_id": 90943,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90943.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 77,
    "turn_text": "Well, first and foremost, what brought out that quote from that book was what I had witnessed. And it wasn't, you know, and it wasn't taken lightly. But what I saw was is from the moment he was elected. I mean, in 2016, there were headlines in paper saying, you know, the countdown to impeachment song. You had Democrats who wasn't in the majority at the time talking about these are impeachable and it just kept building up and it was never something pointed at an act. I believe always the founders had intended this very important check imbalance. I believe it should be used if needed. And it's something Congress must hold sacred. I think too many times in Congress, we take our role and we've given it away. And you brought up, you know, as we've talked about, you know, declaring war. But sometimes we think about that, but yet we also control the purse strings. We can stop or start on any time we want to. But we sort of It's sort of let that go. It's to other agencies. Impeachment is one that we can't let become part of popular fodder. We need to be at a point, and what we saw in this point to take an action against a president who needs to be deliberate and needs to be thoughtful and needs to be investigated, it doesn't need to be simply because we don't like you. And they gave me no reason over the course of this and the hearings and everything to say it was mainly because we just don't like you, Donald Trump. We don't like your style, we don't like your tweets, we don't like who you are. And so when they brought out the impeachment article and why I wrote this was, you have this abuse of power. And a reporter asked me about my opinion of that abuse of power. I said they put that in there because they didn't have anything to quote charge with. But every member of their party could now go, here's what I don't like about Donald Trump, and that's the abuse of power. I didn't like the way he talked about immigrants. I didn't like the way he talked about Congress. I didn't like that. So the abuse of power became generic. Be careful, because, and it's happened in both parties, this word, I don't like what Barack Obama did, so Republicans, let's impeach him. W Bush was in war, let's impeach him. My concern is if it becomes so commonplace, the threats, and especially if it becomes visceral, And what happens when we have someone who needs to really be investigated, who does need to be held accountable by Congress and removed from office? Will there still be the same standard of impeachment or will it be something that the executive branch just dismisses? That's my concern right now.",
