    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90943.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 86,
    "turn_text": "It was, and you've had to live through so much that you just talked about, but I will go back and I'll tell you the basis for it is what I wrote about. And that was if there was a takeaway from this book is this. Our institutions were set up for a purpose. Our institutions were set up in a way that ensured that people were heard. The people we represented should be heard in the halls of Congress. And that means that there's going to be a minority and there's going to be a majority. That's just the way we're set up. Right now we're basically a two-party system and that's what we have. It was never set up for a parliamentary system. It set up for these two parties and the way it has moved forward. Granted, they weren't two parties when it first started. It's developed into that. How are they heard? Well, if I'm in a minority, but our position to be heard, then you have to have a process through committees, through the floor, through our minority leaders and others in which that is expressed. It will never be the majority opinion as long as a majority can hold themselves together. If they can't hold themselves together, then that's a whole definition. Maybe our argument's better, but that's the value of an argument. If we've degraded that, which happened in these hearings, when people saw that it didn't matter if you cut somebody off, if you didn't matter, is I had to take Speaker Pelosi's words down, the Speaker of the House who got on the floor of the House and accused the President of being a criminal. And I had to take her words down when she told me, she just wasn't going to do it, and then had her members come back and overturn the punishment. Where are we going here? That is where we're seeing it. And it's concerning because my concern is that would become the norm. My hope is we put this very bad part in history behind us and we move forward in a deliberative body that actually gets back to having discussions on bills and processes instead of them becoming what you and I were starting to see and that was everything had to be crammed into one big bill or two big bills. You didn't have the stuff in single bills or in processes that reflected what the American people are looking for.",
